4.4. Functions on groups
We model images and stacks of feature maps in a conventional CNN as functions f : Z2 → RK supported on a bounded (typically rectangular) domain. At each pixel coordinate (p, q) ∈ Z2, the stack of feature maps returns a K-dimensional vector f (p, q), where K denotes the number of channels.
Although the feature maps must always be stored in finite arrays, modeling them as functions that extend to infinity (while being non-zero on a finite region only) simplifies the mathematical analysis of CNNs.
We will be concerned with transformations of the feature maps, so we introduce the following notation for a transformation g acting on a set of feature maps:
[Lgf ](x) = [f ◦ g−1](x) = f (g−1x) (4)
Computationally, this says that to get the value of the gtransformed feature map Lgf at the point x, we need to do a lookup in the original feature map f at the point g−1x, which is the unique point that gets mapped to x by g. This


